"""Worked figure examples (D-108 follow-up): a small hand-built, rendered-and-checked figure for each
family of thing, and a short recipe, added to a turn's rules only when the user's message looks
like a request to draw something (`guide_for`). A small model copies an example more faithfully
than it follows a rule, so the example is of the nearest family; it is always told to build the
user's thing from scratch. The figures are the ones `companion/tools/figure-render/replies`
renders (the self-test pins that the two agree), compact so a turn pays for about 700 tokens.
Not in the eval's "wide" set on purpose: those eight things test whether the examples generalise.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import os
import re
from typing import NamedTuple

RECIPE = """
## Building it well
Work in this order: (1) the main body first, whole: one lathe, loft, box or extrude, its long side
along z (nose toward +z) or its height along y; (2) the big parts attached to it; (3) the small
details last. Join parts with from/to or at exact surfaces, so a strut or arm ends where it meets
the body. Use mirror for left/right pairs, copies for rows (windows, ribs) and around for things
spaced round an axis (spokes, blades); never write the same part twice. Don't draw outlines,
shadows, labels or the ground as parts: the card draws those. Show the outside unless the user
asks to see inside. Aim for 12-24 parts at real proportions, two or three colours, and the
details that make this particular thing recognisable. Repeats are costly: every copy, mirror and
around counts, and parts past the limit (about 100 pieces in all) are dropped, so keep
the counts modest and the order of importance first.
A streamlined body (a car, a boat's hull, a fuselage, a fish, a shoe) is a loft: sections
[z,y,w,h,square] from tail to nose along z, each centred at height y, w and h half its width and
height, square 0 for an oval to 1 for a rounded box; 5-9 sections, a 0-size section for a point.
The skin flows smoothly through them, so the profile is in the numbers: a low nose, a high roof.
Organic things (a cell, an organ, an animal's body, fruit, a rock) are blob
parts: a few balls [x,y,z,r] that overlap and melt into one surface; wobble 0.05-0.15 makes it look
alive. A blade, arm, tail or tentacle is a blob with chain:true, its balls in order from thick to
thin. A vessel, stem or horn that narrows is a tube with r2, its end radius. Many small loose
things (organelles, ribosomes, stars, rivets) are one part with scatter:{count,in:[rx,ry,rz]}
round its at; every scattered copy counts. A thing that turns in real life (fan, rotor, wheels,
propeller, turbine) gets spin with its rpm (10-40); blades made with around turn about their hub,
and each wheel turns on its own axle. Any colour: paint:#rrggbb.
Your words explain the thing: what it is, what its parts do, how it works. Don't say you drew or
built it, which way it faces, or which parts are labelled. Do say when it is simplified or
approximate, and what has been cut away to show inside.
"""


class Family(NamedTuple):
    name: str
    words: str      # regex of what, in the user's message, points at this family
    shows: str      # what the example teaches, in a clause
    example: str    # the figure block's JSON, compact


